.TH "OSTRYA\-RECEIVE" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-receive \- Receive one push session over standard input and standard output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya receive [OPTIONS]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Receive one push session over standard input and standard output.
.PP
By default, \fB\%ostrya push\fR runs this command on the server over ssh. The client name is \%\-\-client, else the value of \fB\%LOGNAME\fR, which sshd sets from the password entry of the user. With neither, the command writes an error line and exits \%1 before it opens the repository. At start, the command reads and checks the client groups, the server group, the trust groups, and the key groups of the repository config, and a bad group ends it with status \%1. \fB\%Hello\fR names the namespace of the session: the local refs, or one collection. The session takes the policy of the client group of that namespace that carries the item \fB\%ssh:NAME\fR\&. For a collection, a group that names the collection wins over a group with \fB\%collection=*\fR\&. With no such group, \fB\%Hello\fR gets \fB\%ref\-denied\fR\&. A session of a collection writes the refs under \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR\&. The session uses no \fB\%anonymous\fR and no \fB\%net:\fR item. Standard output carries the frames of the session alone. For each warning of the session, the command writes one \fB\%warning: STEP: MESSAGE\fR line to standard error. It writes nothing else, also under \%\-\-verbose. The exit status is \%0 after a committed session, also with warnings, and \%1 otherwise.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-client\fR \fINAME\fR
The client name of the session, in place of \fB\%LOGNAME\fR\&.
.IP
The session selects the client group that carries the item \fB\%ssh:NAME\fR\&. NAME is not empty, holds no control character, and does not start or end with white space. The option is safe only under a forced \fB\%command=\fR in \fB\%authorized_keys\fR, because a user with a shell can give any name.
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
